# UI Governance

Every QuantaHub UI/UX task MUST begin by reading `docs/ui/README.md` and the documents it marks as canonical.

## Product identity

QuantaHub is a serious cybersecurity learning platform and professional cyber workspace. The product should feel technical, premium, calm, modular, responsive and interactive.

Hack The Box and TryHackMe may be used only as product-reference inspiration. Never copy their layouts, brand colors, wording, illustrations, iconography or distinctive branded components.

Avoid cyber clichés such as Matrix rain, skull imagery, excessive neon/glow, fake decorative terminals, random gradients, emoji as primary icons and cartoon-like gamification.

## Frontend architecture

- Preserve CTFd as the application chassis.
- Prefer CTFd plugin/theme extension, Jinja, existing Vite/Bootstrap conventions, Alpine.js where appropriate, CSS custom properties and progressive enhancement.
- Do not introduce a React/Vue/Next-style SPA merely for visual polish.
- Add a new frontend dependency only when existing project capabilities cannot solve the problem cleanly; verify license, maintenance and bundle impact first.
- Do not bypass platform APIs, authorization or lab/service boundaries for frontend convenience.

## Responsive requirement

Responsive behavior is a hard requirement. Every primary learner flow must work on wide desktop, laptop, tablet and mobile. Critical lab status/actions must never be desktop-only.

## Simulation honesty

M1.5 may use explicit mock providers/adapters for UI development. Simulated terminal, lab and recommendation behavior must be deterministic, clearly distinguishable from live runtime behavior and replaceable without rewriting the UI.

## Accessibility

WCAG AA-oriented contrast, keyboard navigation, visible focus, semantic structure, non-color-only status, reduced-motion support and accessible dynamic status announcements are part of completion, not later polish.
